# The ledger of `lists`: what Go 1.27 (`slices`, `sort`), Rust 1.100 (the slice,
# `Vec`, `Iterator`) and Python 3.15 (`list`, `sorted`, `reversed`, `min`, `max`,
# `sum`, `enumerate`, `zip`, `itertools`, `bisect`) offer for a list, one block
# per capability, and what Heroes answers to each.
#
# Three facts of the language shape every answer. Generics have no constraints,
# so `==`, `<` and `+` are refused on a type parameter: an equality is a
# function the caller gives, `(function(left: T, right: T) -> bool)`, and an
# order is another, `(function(a: T, b: T) -> bool)`, true where `a` goes
# before `b`; the two types differ by their names, so one is never taken for
# the other. There is no generic record and no tuple, so nothing answers two
# values of different types. Every value is a copy, so a function answers a
# new list and no entry that works through a mutable view has a twin here.
#
# Where a line says `built-in` followed by a form that is not a function's
# name (`xs[i]`, `==`, `for`, `=`, `[]`), the language does it with that form.

## What the language does

capability list/length  how many elements a list holds, and whether it holds none
  heroes  built-in len
  go      -
  rust    slice::len, slice::is_empty, Vec::len, Vec::is_empty, Iterator::count
  python  len
  note    an empty list is `xs.len() == 0`

capability list/element  the element at an index, aborting past the end
  heroes  built-in xs[i]
  go      -
  rust    slice::index
  python  xs[i]
  note    Python's negative index counts from the end; here it aborts, and the end is reached through `xs.len()`

capability list/set-element  a list with the element at an index replaced
  heroes  built-in xs[i] @ v
  go      -
  rust    slice::index_mut, slice::get_mut, slice::first_mut, slice::last_mut, slice::iter_mut
  python  xs[i] = v

capability list/append  a list with one more element at its end
  heroes  built-in push
  go      -
  rust    Vec::push
  python  list.append

capability list/slice  the elements between two indices
  heroes  built-in slice
  go      -
  rust    slice::get
  python  xs[a:b], itertools.islice
  note    `slice` aborts past the end where Python cuts short; the forms that cut short are `lists.take` and `lists.skip`

capability list/copy  an independent copy of a list
  heroes  built-in =
  go      slices.Clone
  rust    slice::to_vec, slice::to_owned, slice::into_vec, Vec::clone
  python  list.copy, itertools.tee
  note    every value is a copy (spec § 3), so a binding is one

capability list/empty  a list holding nothing
  heroes  built-in []
  go      -
  rust    Vec::new, Vec::clear, slice::default
  python  list.clear

capability list/walk  each element in turn, stopping where the program says
  heroes  built-in for
  go      slices.Values
  rust    slice::iter, slice::into_iter, Iterator::for_each, Iterator::try_for_each, Iterator::try_fold, Iterator::try_reduce, Iterator::try_find
  python  iter
  note    a `for` whose body answers an error with `?` stops at the first failure, which is what the `try_` forms are for

capability list/map  each element through a function
  heroes  built-in map
  go      -
  rust    Iterator::map, Vec::from_fn
  python  -
  note    `Vec::from_fn` is `map` over `range`

capability list/filter  the elements a function accepts
  heroes  built-in filter
  go      -
  rust    Iterator::filter, Vec::retain, Vec::retain_mut
  python  -

capability list/fold  one value built from every element
  heroes  built-in fold
  go      -
  rust    Iterator::fold, Iterator::reduce, Iterator::sum, Iterator::product
  python  sum
  note    a sum or a product of integers is `math.sum_i64` or `math.product_i64`, and a sum of floats `math.sum`, each failing `does_not_fit` where a fold with `+` aborts; of any other type a sum is a fold with the addition of its own type, which the caller writes because `+` is refused on a type parameter; `reduce` is a fold that starts at the first element

capability list/find  the first element a function accepts
  heroes  built-in find
  go      -
  rust    Iterator::find
  python  -

capability list/any  whether a function accepts some element
  heroes  built-in any
  go      slices.ContainsFunc
  rust    Iterator::any
  python  -

capability list/all  whether a function accepts every element
  heroes  built-in all
  go      -
  rust    Iterator::all
  python  -

capability list/sort-natural  numbers, texts or bools in ascending order
  heroes  built-in sort
  go      slices.Sort, slices.Sorted, sort.Ints, sort.Float64s, sort.Strings
  rust    slice::sort, slice::sort_unstable, slice::sort_floats
  python  sorted, list.sort
  note    `sort` aborts on a `nan`, where Go orders it first and Python leaves the list in no order

capability list/equal  whether two lists hold equal elements in the same order
  heroes  built-in ==
  go      slices.Equal
  rust    slice::eq, slice::ne, Iterator::eq, Iterator::ne
  python  list ==
  note    `==` is refused on a type parameter, so generic code asks `lists.equal_by`

