## Order

capability list/sort-by  a list in the order a function gives, equal elements keeping their places
  heroes  lists.sort_by
  go      slices.SortFunc, slices.SortStableFunc, slices.SortedFunc, slices.SortedStableFunc, sort.Slice, sort.SliceStable, sort.Sort, sort.Stable, sort.Reverse
  rust    slice::sort_by, slice::sort_by_key, slice::sort_by_cached_key, slice::sort_unstable_by, slice::sort_unstable_by_key, slice::select_nth_unstable, slice::select_nth_unstable_by, slice::select_nth_unstable_by_key, slice::partial_sort_unstable, slice::partial_sort_unstable_by, slice::partial_sort_unstable_by_key
  python  sorted, list.sort
  note    always stable, so the unstable forms have no twin. Stability observed against Python's sorted and Go's SortStableFunc on cards of repeated rank, and held over a thousand repeated keys. A `nan` given to an order that compares with `<` aborts, as `<` does on one, where Python leaves such a list in no order. A key is said as an order comparing the two keys, since a key of a type parameter has no `<`; a descending order is an order the caller writes, which keeps equal elements in place as Python's `reverse=True` does. The nth smallest is this then an index, at the sort's cost

capability list/sort-by-with-state  a list in the order a function gives with a value of the caller's, equal elements keeping their places
  heroes  lists.sort_by_with
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `sort_by` with the caller's value handed to every comparison, the field a request asked to sort by or the point distances are measured from, which Go's `slices.SortStableFunc` and Rust's `sort_by` read from a closure and Python's `sorted(key=...)` from a lambda; stable, as `sort_by` is

